Comparison with affine policies
As pointed out earlier affine feedback policies from the noise have been previously treated in {{cite:7551cdbc26f51a2e43e2720884ebee6598d049d6}}, {{cite:0514a6309bce9ae86e718a99d3b9a8dc98c74191}}, {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}}, {{cite:82cfe411ccc51a0355ff154d452782bb8bea316a}}, where the following feedback policy was considered:
{{formula:5dde3d7f-365f-4899-88d9-6449ff1daed4}} 
In the deterministic setting it was shown in {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}} that there exists a one-to-one (nonlinear) mapping between control policies in the form (REF ) and the class of affine state feedback policies. That is, provided one is interested in affine state feedback policies, the parametrization (REF ) constitutes no loss of generality. In fact, we shall illustrate in the examples, in the unconstrained inputs case, that the performance of this strategy with {{formula:cc2ea477-6194-4441-b2cb-7991dbdf1fbc}}  in place of {{formula:594b322f-42b8-4507-bc1c-687756c0c796}}  is almost as good as the standard LQG controller if not equally good.
However, in the constrained inputs case this choice is suboptimal in the class of measurable control policies, but it ensures tractability of a large class of optimal control problems.
It can be seen that the solution to the optimization problem (REF ) is tractable with this parametrization {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}}. However, if the elements of the noise vector {{formula:5f80858d-3bfc-4df7-98bf-79a15740937e}}  are unbounded, the control input (REF ) does not have an upper bound. For the case of bounded inputs, the control policy (REF ) under unbounded noise will in general not satisfy the control constraint sets (REF ). This unboundedness is a potential problem in practical applications, and has been usually circumvented by assuming that the noise input lies within a compact set {{cite:742925a2fdacb7442813e2a5a630b6a3005695e8}}, {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}} and designing a worst-case min-max type controller under this assumption.
It is important to point out that our result in Section REF  differs from that in {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}} in two aspects. First, we are solving the problem on finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces with general basis functions as opposed to a finite collection of affine functions in {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}}. Second, the feasibility of our problem is maintained for any bound on the elements of {{formula:3e0633e3-8480-46b7-bf0a-367a535e1673}} , as our constraint in (REF ) could still produce a feedback gain matrix {{formula:5686dc6f-7e58-43d1-88fe-bb7461408bd5}}  that has norm substantially different that 0, whereas if there are elements in {{formula:6b52deca-6f0e-4069-9949-dcd77a4c6da2}}  with large enough norm and we take the control input to be {{formula:da33bc05-04e3-47a3-97f6-702a7a009c53}} , the constraints produce always a solution {{formula:9c260be6-4023-4253-9fee-520881934fe6}}  with norm very close to 0, hence practically only the open-loop term remains in the case of {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}}.

Stability Analysis
The main result in Theorem REF  asserts that the finite horizon optimization problem (REF ) is convex and tractable using the policy (REF ). To apply this result in a receding horizon fashion, it is imperative to further study some qualitative stability properties of the proposed policy. Under this policy the closed-loop system is not necessarily Markovian, and as such, standard Foster-Lyapunov methods cannot be directly applied. In what follows, we treat the stability problem for {{formula:5c368963-f7df-46e0-82ce-15be6a33340f}}  and {{formula:3c7a3d94-e3ad-457b-928d-988046ced9c6}} . However, this is without any loss of generality, for the same results hold (with minor modifications in the proofs) for {{formula:ab45b075-35e2-4a32-866f-34435d9c4002}}  as well. We impose the following assumption:
Assumption 5.1 
The matrix {{formula:2d93f833-8073-47e8-a7d1-37b2df5821f5}}  in (REF ) is Schur stable, i.e., the absolute value of the eigenvalues of {{formula:4be6767a-ab3e-40dd-9fbb-7abee5949344}}  are all strictly less than 1.{{formula:da61ce76-14c7-4f62-8b8d-318d7048bc01}}

At a first glance this assumption on {{formula:a07444c0-e624-46dd-89cd-7ec187a7bf65}}  might seem restrictive. Indeed, in the deterministic setting we know {{cite:0af206c06c1162f19cd019873755fdb5d3726f62}} that for discrete-time controlled systems it is possible to achieve global asymptotic stability with bounded control inputs if and only if the pair {{formula:f7651e08-d766-427f-bc57-4eb145700c49}}  is stabilizable with arbitrary controls, and the spectral radius of {{formula:b3e24567-6de4-4681-ab30-ba5d3de9b3db}}  is at most 1. However, the problem of ensuring bounded variance of linear stochastic systems with bounded control inputs is to our knowledge still largely open; see, however, the recent manuscript {{cite:12efef00350073c57a6a12df892809539d0abf38}} for partial results as well as in {{cite:cb15e643c62050f5fc5de023adad348902f629c0}}, {{cite:fbe360d211cf2cae09cc21af85c0bb1ac6c583a7}}.
