In other words, we saturate the measurements that we obtain from the noise input vector before inserting them into our control vector. This way we allow that the noise distribution is supported over the entire {{formula:3dd7901c-9da9-4d52-aabf-d65a44487f0d}} , which is an advantage over other approaches {{cite:742925a2fdacb7442813e2a5a630b6a3005695e8}}, {{cite:78551d156311c38bfd4a9ecc77bbc794516ae22a}}. Moreover, the choice of the component saturation function {{formula:bd076a07-5daf-492f-86eb-163cc8c65259}}  is left open as long as the noise sequence satisfies Assumption REF . For example, we can accommodate standard saturation, piecewise linear, and sigmoidal functions to name a few.

Our choice of saturating the measurement from the noise vectors, as we shall see below, renders the resulting optimization problem tractable as opposed to calculating the entire control input vector {{formula:c2b0d677-26b1-4a07-be04-9366bce3808a}}  and then saturating it a posteriori; one can see that the latter approach tends to lead to an intractable optimization problem. Note also that the choice of control inputs in (REF ) yields a possibly non-Markovian feedback.

