In this section we present several numerical examples to illustrate the theoretical results in the preceding sections. We start in Example REF  by comparing the performance of our policy (REF ) to that of the standard finite horizon LQG controller whenever the control inputs set {{formula:d4490590-b567-4abf-9d3f-40011628fe54}} , i.e., there are no bounds on the norm of the inputs. Then we compare the performance of our policy (REF ) against a saturated LQG controller in Example REF . Finally, in Example REF  we illustrate the effectiveness of our policy (REF ) compared to the certainty-equivalent receding horizon control.

Example 6.1 (Unconstrained Inputs) 

A natural question that may arise whenever the control inputs in our setup are not constrained, i.e., {{formula:30de1813-1894-4d08-99dd-1a6883d05b40}} , is the following: How does the policy (REF ) compare to the globally optimal controller, which in this case is the standard finite-horizon LQG controller? One would expect our policy to perform worse on the average since we restrict to a class of feedback policies that may not contain the globally optimal controller.

We compared our policy against that of the LQG problem in simulation for two controllable 3-dimensional single-input linear systems. In each case we solved an unconstrained finite-horizon LQ optimal control problem corresponding to state and control weights {{formula:9c00190b-c809-48cf-a307-366eaca59ce3}}  and {{formula:b378e31c-c84e-43c2-96f1-45e2f278ee8d}}  for every {{formula:c2d9635b-e594-4dd6-a514-dc038092a086}} . We selected an optimization horizon {{formula:92be554c-99df-4eaa-bb9c-8244ffe56ea5}} , and simulated the system responses starting from {{formula:b9ebde6e-3438-4384-8325-36b2e8fc525a}}  different initial conditions {{formula:752acb97-02f2-46de-8e03-d6b753a8bc25}}  selected at random uniformly from the cube {{formula:18def2cf-a0e4-4256-8a57-1e6f40013093}} , and noise sequences {{formula:b3a5bcce-8923-4ab7-a29c-afeff1d1ef79}}  corresponding to i.i.d Gaussian noise of mean 0 and (randomly chosen) variance
