Hence, the surface of the orbit is not only conserved but also quantized: it is proportional to the Hamiltonian and cannot change continuously (an equivalent observation see, e.g. {{cite:c0ece773b75010af48820aa7f09c45bc9ab06f45}}).

As it seems, this is not the first time when the magnetic fields provide an imitation of still unchecked theories. The suggestive ideas of supersymmetry are still not verified in particle physics. Yet, due to the anomalous relation between the spin and orbital magnetic moments of the electron, its energy levels in a static, homogeneous magnetic field reproduce the supersymmetric spectrum. So, many authors conclude that: “the supersymmetry exists in nature” {{cite:313690acc7b3279b8de7c084b5f03c5e04521521}}, {{cite:21e7c130a3883dd040b6e27be771c72d545f711b}}, {{cite:7dfd99b66ba91c01b5bd38dad67807f622344dae}}, {{cite:f2b3a7972368b726b256a58c90b8eba561751308}}. Quite similarly, there is no evidence that the physical particles move in non-commuting spaces, but the properties of the rotation center (REF ) might encourage the statement that “the non-commutative positions exist in nature”.

It might be thus interesting to notice that the phenomenon is not limited to the circular trajectories in the static magnetic fields. It appears as well in more general physical scenarios, including the time dependent oscillator potentials and magnetic fields.

The evolution problem for variable oscillator potentials has already a notable past. The first systematic studies were presented in 1967-69 by Lewis and Riesenfeld {{cite:6cb8bb84a48aaa0c03f9afb83880e4e5287964c2}}, {{cite:1da1723c8a1dad82f7fb38de29af4d933fd06b89}}, then by Malkin and Man'ko {{cite:fd38af714f3f8f77d9cc4a7d508193c50ebce228}}, inspiring an ample research on the state evolution. The group theoretical approach to the Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff (BCH) problem {{cite:f4815b8c947a38b138c69006aa269452174f805e}}, {{cite:ba90d84985edb784cf632ce7ccc23942c08bd2c9}}, {{cite:2459fee6f9c15a6ccef6319f89f8f36bbd0ccab8}}, {{cite:8a684fb1add8db4c96ddd433c246a51cae041355}} for quadratic Hamiltonians was developed in 1974 by Gilmore {{cite:4453a71acbf785ad3ea7f5dd94d0fb786af9bdf6}} (a generalization see Zhang et al {{cite:6134a7d255ccbd1c718681e78f21eb2739aa9c1e}}). In 1976 Yuen notices the possibility of solving the evolution equation for the squeezed photon states {{cite:c834dd27ae4cf77d507921d167ceb842340193e4}}. For massive charged particles the operations induced by variable fields were studied by Ma and Rhodes {{cite:4b1e40da600449036d7d31bf925db4e9b29009df}}, Royer {{cite:99cbb8e8d8fa8eb87f18494a8d8a7a01a7700159}}, Brown and Carson {{cite:8060674121c0d956a93ec2eba008cd820da83dc4}}, Combescure {{cite:9f7a0004c525e67c0981d1eeb7ade5d168e9f6fe}}, Wolf {{cite:143dd03f6c07eff3858ba06ed3c7fd6df1e54ab7}} and other authors; for an ample review c.f. Dodonov {{cite:c42d0bebedfa85446d35f51e5557d1e2dc3e347e}}. The links with the coherent states are notable {{cite:210c1595af10e9d7f59def9efe2268b14092c22a}}, {{cite:cb935afbdfa7c2f245ba39554f2c7f4d784206f1}}, {{cite:0bf4c405e5723dfd3519ef69e2b99e304b099902}}, {{cite:4ff7c923cfd84b4fe89b68746ddff7ddd0214479}}. In the parallel research there was hardly any constant in the generalized oscillator or magnetic Hamiltonians which would not be replaced by its time dependent analogue, starting from the time dependent mass ({{formula:2f30a013-606b-49ad-94d3-b5213389aa55}}  the Hamiltonian of Caldirola-Kanai {{cite:1d006b8f7ce974f51194aeff2f6b840f013136ec}}, {{cite:6bb277f7790dcb737dda25395bcce529dfdadf73}}, {{cite:4be3c4a3642e5899f80acbb8894979676aa50d26}}) up to the variable dielectric or magnetic permeability {{cite:68d8f2b2e655077902fff0a1f9e588538c0faf6f}}, {{cite:ea486eccf4b3735aa751aa652cdaa5a43695e95e}}, {{cite:ae741d7bdf9ca7ba72ad5f2eeb69818deda0dafb}}, {{cite:822177862a7d39679d1f82888be30c583c381d57}}.

