Some incomplete versions of the loop process may prove of interest. In fact, whenever an evolution loop contains a {{formula:c44fa1c5-5010-4bc1-8316-15a2dcc0734f}} -pulse of the attractive oscillator potential, as in (REF ), the whole rest of the process must imitate the effect of a repulsive elastic pulse (which cannot be straightforwardly achieved by the magnetic fields). Furthermore, if any evolution loop contains an interval of free evolution {{formula:12257900-79ad-4a3b-b398-24d9e27d6c3d}}  {{formula:4b8448ec-1099-4dd7-9f9c-0c16978d1522}} , it means that the whole rest must be equivalent to its inverse, thus suggesting the techniques of reverting the free propagation. The importance of the closed dynamical processes for more general control operations was recognized in {{cite:42793cdcfd9fd0fbedaa111e0a24dc3798519cc7}}, {{cite:c2202c96616f95dd125659717c056987dd08d091}}, {{cite:510adfad308a8ebe458370e364db8754ddca249f}}, {{cite:b92b3510836eb240856e231eb19acd2fa4761234}}, {{cite:82fdd1f85b643b77a27aed18de20e247d1be27e9}}.

All these models depend on some idealizations. Thus, the repulsive oscillator kicks cannot be straightforwardly simulated by the magnetic fields (in fact, even the attractive ones can hardly be engineered!). Moreover, in order to describe the time dependent potentials in some little but macroscopic areas as, {{formula:c66ee987-937e-40f9-9127-435fa7ed278b}} , an ion trap, one typically uses the non-relativistic laboratory approximation, disregarding the little delays needed to propagate the potential inputs all over the trap surfaces (telegraphist's equations) or in its interior. Since these delays in typical experiments are insignificant, the lab approximation works very well and indeed, is implicit in all papers postulating the time dependent external parameters ({{formula:86621401-7741-4983-adf3-896de8dcaf28}}  {{cite:6cb8bb84a48aaa0c03f9afb83880e4e5287964c2}}, {{cite:fd38af714f3f8f77d9cc4a7d508193c50ebce228}}, {{cite:bc4913a0057979f1d0fed5c60e31bb7a7bf54175}}, {{cite:a53331ca777725bfcde9f2d421574affc3f7722f}}, {{cite:fbb5c7fd7c30bed4e1dbf75b2e723d4726730733}}, {{cite:68d8f2b2e655077902fff0a1f9e588538c0faf6f}}, {{cite:210c1595af10e9d7f59def9efe2268b14092c22a}}).

Once this approximation is adopted, one can see that the fuzzy centers
are not restricted to the orthodox Hall effect with a fixed magnetic background. They arise, with comparable consequences, for arbitrary evolution loops generated by the static or time dependent quadratic Hamiltonians.

To show this, we shall consider the hermitian Hamiltonians {{formula:c294e3ee-a471-4b02-8aa7-21d348f526cb}}  defined by time dependent quadratic forms {{formula:f05c2ef0-21d1-46e0-a09e-dc1515cc40f0}}  (with {{formula:ccc54450-5216-4786-9b67-a3bcf939b0d9}} ) of a complete set {{formula:95296d4d-f4ad-4cbd-8bbc-2fafb8388d30}}  of any number of the canonical observables, {{formula:caa6caa3-4e49-4382-ac93-7eaa4aabc9a4}}  {{formula:956c8977-d937-44e8-9cba-2264b669dd9d}}  in a certain Hilbert space {{formula:faa534c0-ba87-4381-8dd0-095f741184ef}} ; {{formula:79a0f76c-6b2c-4030-8483-63a3d4e5d064}}  {{formula:78a87831-fca5-411f-85a8-a0995783c438}} . An agreeable property of the quadratic Hamiltonians is that even though {{formula:d55b10a0-8585-4fba-82e7-8239eb1229aa}}  are unbounded, but if the coefficients {{formula:c3bebb04-3667-4354-9fb1-c6ebad73d600}}  are non-singular and piece-wise continuous, the corresponding unitary evolution operators {{formula:0a48cd94-916b-4b5f-9bde-b729d8446051}}  are well defined by the operator equations:
