We shall show now, that the behavior of the loop affected by an additional force described in Secs. - is a typical phenomenon for all loop processes generated by any quadratic Hamiltonians.

Proposition 1 Suppose, a time-periodic Hamiltonian {{formula:0484a6fb-79f8-4a3c-abdd-f2d1bf3f2c87}} , quadratic in the canonical observables {{formula:abb968b2-df7f-4e4a-b893-6c0790a641dc}} , generates a loop (REF ) in its periodicity intervals {{formula:4c40360f-34a0-4d51-9521-2aa5288cb720}} , {{formula:eab3e8cc-60af-46e5-a15d-6270981068b3}} . Then the precession or stability of the loop under an additional, constant force {{formula:37956122-0e59-4506-93d8-ce3a29d79a8a}}  depends on the loop center {{formula:8a1dae65-7e39-4010-b9f7-0161517ddf29}} . If its coordinates vanish, then the loop can change its shape but remains closed and stable. If the coordinates commute, then the center is stable even if the trajectory is not. However, if {{formula:5fdbd4e7-275d-423e-8a95-5c186ae00e9a}}  is a “fuzzy point” with non-commuting coordinates, {{formula:c0c4ac04-884d-44ab-871c-9e9707880920}} , then the loop will show a drift with a constant velocity {{formula:3da808da-77df-48ef-8f0b-db4e847d4195}}  (summation convention) in the direction orthogonal to {{formula:dd059332-d71b-4c08-92ab-511f88cf744f}} .

