Our results permit to classify the loops according to the properties of {{formula:0905c23e-cf0a-47b0-b392-bf2293e834c1}} :

A loop generated by a quadratic Hamiltonian for {{formula:deb5b150-a47b-4710-aeeb-66baf4ab5503}}  can have a `fuzzy center' {{formula:901d408d-15f2-4ba6-9900-11a67c79459e}}  with non-trivial {{formula:eaec51d8-f042-402f-918e-e6c0c69d1bdc}} . Then under the influence of a constant external force {{formula:83a40b22-1104-463a-87f2-5a752e500bca}}  the loop can be broken; the former motion center will drift with a constant velocity in a direction orthogonal to the applied force.

If {{formula:0ef15f20-5e36-4f58-b6ef-f7b4b2f39fe5}} , then the loop center {{formula:b76e743a-32d3-40a0-9b17-a49c33735c98}} , no matter the external force, must return to its initial value. Various subcases are still possible.

If {{formula:f8ce54a8-2b25-4755-b4ef-1854684a760c}}  does not vanish identically, the loop still can be broken. The motion center won't drift but the trajectory can precess around the fixed center {{formula:09d4ef0c-97f0-4c23-b4d7-f7f513eba4d1}} . The detailed classification of the loops in this subcase is still an open problem.

If {{formula:29bc763b-938e-4870-b860-135ebf6aaac8}}  identically vanishes, then {{formula:7674d03d-e470-4dc7-9e40-cd51efcdeaf6}}  in (REF ) is just a phase factor and the loop is stable: it can be deformed but it won't be broken, neither will it precess under the influence of {{formula:9cefcfc9-7ed8-4732-9e42-1ff3d7f8e7e5}} .

The sense of this classification turns obvious if one compares the traditional harmonic oscillator with the “magnetic oscillator” {{formula:3e3369ea-2ad1-429b-9481-0ee682c5f755}} constant). Both admit circular orbits, however, the general elliptic orbit of the oscillator affected by an external force {{formula:7815c314-1f3a-462d-9a7a-90169af2da7c}}  gets simply displaced in the direction of {{formula:5cfcbbbd-408d-4b7b-934e-ffb39af38513}}  (where it remains stationary), while the orbit of {{formula:a7c5c72c-9b35-4ff7-aa29-53b543b35c3b}}  starts drifting in the direction orthogonal to {{formula:6f94bd1e-fadd-40dd-8acc-3463ef9e0cb6}} . The key to this difference is the distinct nature of the classical/quantum center {{formula:0bc83ab2-a633-4eef-a8c3-4afab173b0f8}}  of both motions. While for the {{formula:943d0a77-442a-4cef-9ab6-fa76ad4a2922}}  oscillator the center {{formula:33188f38-f635-4704-bc90-950de722f993}}  is exactly zero, the same center for {{formula:5eeb3923-761e-457b-935a-553fc42b3da8}}  is a fuzzy point (REF )-() implying the drifting trajectory.

In physical terms, the difference between both cases has some historical key. In fact, by reading (REF ) inversely: {{formula:c60bd4a8-705a-4f1b-af69-b7ce0221484b}} , one obtains a modern equivalent of an old idea: the description of the elliptic orbit of {{formula:6200ce7c-8a55-4cdb-b523-8d3dd9e276ae}}  as the superposition of two circular motions, for {{formula:68a0e699-ace6-422f-a726-aa5fc9ffbcaf}}  and {{formula:fc020782-eec3-4938-841c-230add2cbd92}} , {{formula:e7b5f885-719e-4837-a20e-359b747051f5}}  the Ptolemean picture of the oscillator trajectory. So, in a sense, “the epicycles are more stable than cycles”.

As already known, the magnetic operations can produce special effects such as the rigid displacement, squeezing, and the distorted free evolution {{cite:bc4913a0057979f1d0fed5c60e31bb7a7bf54175}}, {{cite:c42d0bebedfa85446d35f51e5557d1e2dc3e347e}}, {{cite:6ddfc41f823f18641364e8e97afa600ad65483d7}}, {{cite:c5c21fc6062761666ea8a239a42e7c2ce7670af6}}. Below, we shall be specially interested in the homogeneous magnetic fields in a fixed direction (e.g. of {{formula:be3f236e-8000-4511-afc3-1d902b78a606}} -axis), imitating the variable oscillator potentials in 2D {{cite:d40bf6843008c551e01aba399245eb193e0eea2f}}, {{cite:c5c21fc6062761666ea8a239a42e7c2ce7670af6}}, {{cite:a53331ca777725bfcde9f2d421574affc3f7722f}}, {{cite:bc4913a0057979f1d0fed5c60e31bb7a7bf54175}}, {{cite:ae9d29f209391cd816e4dbf5f5ac7333f3704d1d}}.
The simplest physical conditions to approximate such fields arise in space domains surrounded by time dependent currents, e.g., by solenoids of various forms (Fig. REF a,b).
{{figure:83d8a2cd-e01f-44fc-b498-e7b6165c6399}}

