In the best known case of ion traps with the sinusoidally oscillating elastic force the solutions {{formula:89cf7bfc-d39b-45c5-9f4a-7ede29b15aa7}}  are expressed in terms of the Mathieu functions and the resonance borders form the well known Strutt diagramme (see e.g.{{cite:a04fc31ac6758c96b2b8841e7ff68117fa1735fa}}, {{cite:587d9db1c1fcda43af365367c4647fc2cf67b96c}}). Due to its familiar shape, most of studies illustrating the effects of the time dependent elastic potentials stick to the Mathieu scheme (see {{formula:d260192b-c9c2-4043-afd5-9fdde72bc730}}  Paul's trap {{cite:136be79a34b2f37ebf5419f44902c31a342b4bc5}}, the quantum tomography {{cite:210c1595af10e9d7f59def9efe2268b14092c22a}}, etc.). The need of some wider stability designs was pointed out by Glauber (c.f. the statement in Baseia et al {{cite:0a511cd4fd9da4ca7cb15c4d3eb75eaf8719744b}}). Indeed, some distinct stability cases were considered for finite dimensional state spaces {{cite:2f1b0850247e6be8a73fe63f0961885dce8ed492}}, {{cite:5512d659ce5467afae04155c4690ac975b66d6b7}}, {{cite:9a3df427ac40e417cf5a7ddecf8fa13a9cfaed2b}}, or else, for the rotating magnetic {{cite:22bc9f04380d7eb4ffeecaa257873ee3236d6118}}, {{cite:de9d1f95cc423961fc32b54a3c84521e56bcf0c6}}, {{cite:eb21a9795149f34ae226ae1be2b25ac6f5f9bfa3}} or electric fields {{cite:d77ed5f13a593a7e21968459242c44568d1c44fc}}, {{cite:b1f4821306ebe663766e58137fb4f6648e15a965}}. However, this does not include the magnetic oscillators {{formula:e5a15905-6735-4d0d-b436-e909d895d169}}  (REF ) modulated by {{formula:5dd4d619-4e07-4f16-b6a7-dc08b53458d1}} . The difference seems modest but the consequences are not.

