While the existence of the harmonic loops is known {{cite:ae9d29f209391cd816e4dbf5f5ac7333f3704d1d}}, their extremely regular, kaleidoscopic forms have some more implications. One of them is the exact vanishing of the “operator centers” {{formula:eb1c3869-2008-41db-b1c1-456f5cec2274}} . Indeed:

Proposition 2 Suppose, for a pair of amplitudes {{formula:e6123d9c-42bc-4900-90df-8929a406ab79}}  the evolution loop of the oscillatory part {{formula:c2a42c5e-c333-4c1c-94ca-3650dbc8831b}}  closes for {{formula:8a1844c4-a844-4d11-85d4-8b345143d02f}} , while the rotation {{formula:6723b544-0d32-4f6d-bda4-4fab9cbc0aea}}  yields a certain non-trivial angle {{formula:36a63ae2-0476-4079-9f28-88cad95aad64}} , where {{formula:6e604c0c-bbe6-4066-903a-33d0b5652db2}}  is rational but not an integer (no matter, whether {{formula:ea6a0c19-b4c7-45c3-9c51-3837680a8ada}}  is smaller or greater than 1). Then, the loop obtained for {{formula:539243b0-4175-4db3-8e8a-7d2cfcad203c}}  by simultaneous closing of both, oscillatory and rotational motions has the trivial center on both classical and quantum levels.

{{figure:f360c43d-c205-4fab-8271-6aafff756171}}

Proof. In fact, the rotation {{formula:48ee793d-ac07-4461-a721-4bf1ff8fffb1}}  breaks the oscillatory loop at {{formula:c9a38364-11e1-48a0-ae89-0bf1d74ae0f9}} , marking a new end point {{formula:7d32b438-802d-44a1-8f5f-25dd683825cb}}  rotated by {{formula:7a53b27c-5654-4b07-b5ba-3dfda3424cf9}}  with respect to the initial {{formula:c64f6980-2598-4277-aa76-4daa1c38ecdb}} . The {{formula:d45ef740-20ac-4700-87d1-afc27615b2fd}} , in turn, becomes the initial point for the next fragment of the trajectory, which again does not close, but is just the {{formula:fff5be81-ab77-42df-b4e8-e840470bef50}} -rotated version of the previous one, ending up at {{formula:4e5dda97-73a1-47e4-acf2-2d888afe7fdc}} , etc. As the result, the whole trajectory is the sum of similar fragments, generated between the time moments {{formula:9ab6f144-ec51-438b-a60d-81290566d6fb}}  joining the subsequent points {{formula:ccb82961-eb78-41bf-a078-f2cfadf7236c}} , each one just the {{formula:b83df157-b3a7-4fd0-8443-636ddb7b070a}} -rotated version of the previous one, until finally, at {{formula:7aaeb032-d4dd-4939-b587-2dc25e1f135b}} , the trajectory closes with {{formula:d114e67e-d1b6-41c8-b1df-59427124d412}} .
As the sum of the {{formula:fac614a9-6b9e-4904-8f67-13911b42bc8d}} -rotated steps, the whole loop is invariant under the {{formula:5a019500-af52-4f8d-a188-bc60a39dee8b}} -rotation and so is the loop center {{formula:0bdb051a-f20a-4670-b124-469d4f2d4005}}  defined by (REF ). However, the only vector (with numerical or operator components) invariant under a non-trivial rotation around the coordinate center is {{formula:3dd02fcd-69d0-49b1-b909-70cd41d5287c}} .{{formula:ba6e1d0d-f3fb-4a61-a6b5-7480096a523a}}

Consistently with Sec.  this assures the stability of the harmonic loops which resist drifting (see Fig. REF ). It looks almost as an elementary analogue of the anomalous resistance observed in {{formula:4246bc9a-48f4-48bd-9c84-2ebf6399fa61}}  {{cite:181f75a61b460a8724ce408794e2eb62d3afcb2b}} (though the attempts of overestimating the analogy might be misleading).

In turn, we shall consider the biharmonic fields () or (REF ) with {{formula:67804bd6-c9fb-4c23-9ed0-3f09a4f22212}} , defined by pairs of the dimensionless amplitudes {{formula:315866fe-f4df-4698-a51e-f5d6283224f3}} . Their agreeable property is that at each {{formula:8701e7c0-11b6-42b6-9b3e-be76e692b538}}  all rotations cancel, and simultaneously, the field amplitudes {{formula:7f144c03-1b03-4961-a8cd-a9eacb677b7c}} . Henceforth, the evolution operators {{formula:e3086a0e-eb25-4432-8fe0-9f104e0d89dd}}  are reduced to the purely oscillatory part; the corresponding one-period evolution matrix (REF ) reduces to a pair of {{formula:70662385-4fc2-473e-879d-b7419606ff44}}  cells. Moreover, the physical sense of the corresponding evolution in the time moments {{formula:26fd536e-1b47-4185-8ec2-309ca462c8b7}}  {{formula:54bddcd2-85a2-4cae-b6c4-3b3a30c35c5d}}  is not affected by the difference between the canonical and kinetic momenta.

The stability areas determined by the computer scanning of {{formula:5d793df4-d32e-478f-8301-b1898377ffbf}}  (c.f. {{cite:e95bd369265d564a9134d95bc54b5625f5fd55e4}}) form now a new 2-parameter map (see Fig. ).
{{figure:0583cd2f-a020-413c-8432-c52a91773a93}}

As before,they are densely populated by curves {{formula:247b56cb-d1fc-4da5-891e-b10f83baad61}} , whose points {{formula:1d9e15aa-ce3e-450c-a891-ef67d18f1d8e}}  generate loops, though now {{formula:cc14b5b9-2379-4dbe-87f1-601b374b6311}}  don't need to fulfill any extra condition as the rotations {{formula:78a6bc83-2c90-4401-80db-47443d41ad75}}  automatically cancel for {{formula:aa242850-91c8-4f8d-a6d8-13c62fc9ca52}} . A loop which closes up after 6 field periods is drawn on Fig. REF . Note its symmetry under the parity reflection, which grants the exact vanishing of {{formula:b1e9d1b5-741f-4518-b6d4-7d44684fab73}} . However the parity is not even necessary.

Proposition 3 Consider a periodic sequence of field pulses {{formula:3c159df9-b3c6-4662-9aa3-4528a222ff94}} , with {{formula:04cf72e9-91b5-47a9-89f0-491190c2bcf4}}  for {{formula:5eb45b4e-b251-4085-86aa-0e74c74edc1e}} , and moreover, {{formula:5b3d816c-0e66-4542-bff5-e90e77b01a58}}  ({{formula:aa3215bb-4d1c-4521-873e-60a34f1a783c}} , the rotations cancel in each full period {{formula:ce7a41d1-05b8-48ba-9285-75fa91299f6b}}  and its multiples). Then the evolution loops which close for the first time after a certain number {{formula:344f524d-5742-4fe7-81c4-e2d66e66df65}}  of the {{formula:2b9f73f4-bb96-4f0b-9f64-63a96fe843bf}} -periods has the vanishing center {{formula:50d9eef4-5ecd-45d6-97a3-8a2d9a9533e3}} .

