If now {{formula:bf0928a8-142d-4044-8f21-ec6b66846e3f}} , then the whole matrix trajectory is invariant under the multiplication by {{formula:e01e47ee-3304-4136-83e1-3474e84f3ea7}}  and so is the trajectory center {{formula:9822a17f-9e1f-46fd-a1da-c3f49a51437b}}  which must fulfill {{formula:9ae2defc-0dad-46ea-93b0-483622d8b1fd}} ; but since {{formula:4350b747-3641-463d-b575-7e4765e7bd00}}  has no eigenvalue 1, the center must vanish identically, implying the loop stability (the loop resists the external forces).{{formula:81a5b480-211b-4bff-aea9-fe41842f43ce}}

The group theoretical sense of the {{formula:7165a5da-2c68-45f6-bdb5-766c84777a47}} -loops can be noticed by reinterpreting the time variable {{formula:05dc139c-0c9c-45cb-b4f6-952dfd716c01}}  as the rotation angle in the plane. The quantity which returns to its initial value after a full {{formula:1ad47eba-c3fb-45f4-8c4f-a03377d615d4}} -rotation is a tensor. An entity which changes its sign is a spinor. An entity which returns to its initial value only after a finite number of {{formula:3de6d368-bb5c-4ef9-bb92-99c957584862}} -rotations (as e.g. the loops of Figs. REF  and REF ) obeys a fractional representation of the rotation group in {{formula:907b4212-c55d-4e20-8e31-8ca53f3240f0}}  {{cite:60a1ba030bd361b18290c6c4ab9a8c48a365bc1a}}.

To estimate the corrections to the scheme it is convenient to return to the ordinary units.
For slow classical/quantum motions (low kinetic momenta), the use of the Schrödinger's QM seems justified; but some dissipative correction must affect our semiclassical results. If the pulsating field is exactly periodic, the probability of quantum jumps might be read from the quasienergies of the Floquet Hamiltonian. Yet, for the low frequency of the applied fields and the evolution limited to a few pulse periods, more reliable seems the Abraham-Lorentz radiative acceleration {{formula:5f03d012-6381-4033-9616-6ea60a92169e}} , where {{formula:ee585c73-f1de-4592-91d2-b906ff7ea78b}}  is the “characteristic time” of the charged particle (c.f. Jackson {{cite:7fc70b2c343a736c96e6bbd2283755373369a11e}}). Taking as the model the electron with {{formula:42ac7873-2d49-4bbc-bae8-dcdb922ff512}}  one has in the dimensionless variables: {{formula:41e14aed-75f2-4301-952a-c25638b85e9a}} ; so in the harmonic field frequency corresponding to the long {{formula:d81583e3-6e51-4ffc-9308-41d486bc475a}}  RW the single operation period {{formula:2b504c14-d876-4465-bc12-629bb9f60c99}}  implying very little A-L corrections {{formula:80fd7b92-e602-4a73-97bb-19d406ebb85f}}  (though the evaluation changes for increasing {{formula:235c1a29-5986-4c14-a7df-d48ccdad1daa}} ; see also {{cite:587d9db1c1fcda43af365367c4647fc2cf67b96c}}).

A different source of errors are the retardation free vector potentials in the laboratory approximations (REF ). Their relativistic equivalents can be obtained by looking for the freely propagating fields of cylindrical symmetry:
