The d'Alembert eq. {{formula:f2a52e61-ef69-4e54-930b-4f992df4eef5}}  then traduces into:
{{formula:f7da5b2e-15e6-4794-8f47-a400b8ff2fc0}}

(compare {{cite:440aac45daa40aaf06ee4d7be460fecf9b10e757}}) which admits an analytic solution {{formula:01238c1d-ed9b-41fe-b1ab-2dc5046d1591}}  with the coefficients given by the recurrence {{formula:fa7e4f45-dc4f-4559-a487-552eb05de7ce}} , {{formula:cd60557a-968c-4bb2-9e49-fc7b0821f103}} . They show that the “laboratory Hamiltonian” (REF -REF ) is once again the first step of the EIH method {{cite:7eccc5f63277fa108d00e2d0815ebdb4cc795dbf}} for the potential (REF -REF ). Its precision depends on the ratio between the effective size {{formula:2ac657c0-798e-464b-9595-671012384f1d}}  of each loop and the wavelength associated with the pulse frequency {{formula:fc1cb308-bdc8-4503-bf0a-9a0f0f6010f2}} , {{formula:c71581b4-c916-4e2c-a1c3-3965497f3720}}  {{formula:07125330-c126-4fec-9ef7-5823da1f3300}} . If {{formula:e6994279-7b49-466c-8eb5-28b850d882bb}}  is much greater than the loop size, {{formula:e8458bab-7336-48c7-80cd-119a0dbb0106}} , then the formula (REF ) describes the harmonic magnetic fields differing little from the homogeneous field (), drawing with a good accuracy the regular loop forms predicted by the quadratic Hamiltonian (REF ). Note that an analogous estimation (of the ratio between the trajectory size and the field inhomogeneity parameter) works also in different circumstances, {{formula:7a0fc034-b8d2-4bd1-84e4-34d5168b35ad}}  for the atomic or molecular systems irradiated by the coherent laser light. The typical atomic size ({{formula:e1c4271e-0670-47b4-a0b9-0f84a8802f51}}  of widely studied {{formula:836ff635-49cf-4954-9546-a662adb97dae}}  atoms) is in the range {{formula:c1345659-88df-40f2-b038-605dc552d7c2}}  but the {{formula:b6ff8be0-ad85-4511-99c6-495b0253abd6}}  of visible light is much greater (between {{formula:56175ce6-281f-4f76-973b-86319f26e81d}}  and {{formula:29fe738f-73a1-4919-9b09-94c6d1a2f97a}} ); which seems one of the reasons why the approximate description of the electric forces by a homogeneous, pulsating field {{formula:aa6aca7e-5961-4999-bed7-de3da87408d9}}  gives so good results in the description of Rabi rotations {{cite:3d71b8b496110d5d6e077b995970f6d36f8399ec}}. The question whether the effects we described can have some analogues for the exact relativistic solution in strongly inhomogeneous fields (e.g. {{cite:c73c892c0bcc95d150de43b2ed6beb1050fc52d8}}) is open.

Until now, our exploration was limited to the stability areas with their typical “fauna” and “flora” of drifting and stable loops. However, the problems of quantum control unavoidably leads us across the borders.

Since the rotations commute with the whole Hamiltonian (REF ), we shall again fix attention on the {{formula:c445f71d-8ec7-447f-a8ef-4bf856205847}}  cells of the evolution process (REF ).

One of the major control challenges are the idealized {{formula:5a11e1fa-5f34-47cc-b576-77cdb04ae06b}} -kicks of the elastic potentials and the events of the “distorted free evolution”, both represented by extremely simple operators and {{formula:3c241ab7-c5ef-42ee-98ac-80710b21dc5e}} -transformations represented by {{formula:748b1837-71f7-4e6b-ab99-8d617f757882}} :
{{formula:58fc1f30-03fc-4a40-ab11-4f69e4b3fdb9}}

(where {{formula:6517dc95-be62-4383-a24e-15908a736f02}} , {{formula:52680676-6b85-4d02-b8b5-1412c7389dca}}  mean {{formula:bd8489f1-bd68-49e8-b1a5-cfe0521c5bff}}  or {{formula:3ccdef85-8bc9-4260-b42c-23cf2f3e2090}} ) though almost impossible to achieve straightforwardly.

The left operation in (REF ) can be interpreted as a formal result of an infinitely strong pulse of an oscillator potential applied within an infinitesimal time. The right one, if {{formula:a9945f65-3105-4722-bd21-c88c56f875eb}} , {{formula:ccf35aec-6530-4f79-a6de-206338b81db5}} , represents the distorted (slowed or accelerated) free evolution; but if {{formula:041695cd-be1a-4af4-ac26-2c7141fe99c2}} , it yields highly counter-intuitive effect: it inverts the free evolution of a wave packet, including the interference between the different packet components. No less provocative are the evolution operators and matrices:
{{formula:78788283-d19c-48e8-a22e-72da7cf43443}}

{{formula:978c90f0-4f13-4eec-8711-8c7d4248b7cc}}  (REF ) superposed with the parity reflection {{formula:5867f63a-fcc2-416b-a604-3cca80f7387c}} . All matrices (REF ), (REF ) belong to the resonance borders (case II of our classification). In principle, they can be generated by combinatorial field patterns {{cite:bc4913a0057979f1d0fed5c60e31bb7a7bf54175}}, {{cite:c5c21fc6062761666ea8a239a42e7c2ce7670af6}}, {{cite:649519ab70d619edf46b5aca0d9b1448b3ee3f90}}. However could they be caused by a “soft persuasion” instead of brutal force?

With some surprise we found that (REF ) and/or (REF ) occur on the separatrix branches of Fig.  with either {{formula:ace9dceb-d5ca-4a0a-97a4-b717f23eb939}}  (we call them of type {{formula:6ba70a06-1fc9-4dce-9c47-446ff919fd2a}} ), or {{formula:e35505f0-256d-41ce-ade3-fda4bd45654e}}  (type {{formula:27984107-4ab6-4487-9b39-3ae6f6a95d67}} ). The result contained some mystery. Though it is quite obvious that for {{formula:ba3d71f7-11b5-4326-b186-d35af6bdd3ca}} , the matrix {{formula:ee48734f-7510-4cb7-b50d-eb5d9e4b6636}}  degenerates, this still does not imply that it must appear in the standard Jordan's form (REF ) or (REF ) precisely in the {{formula:4b1be663-0d4a-4ac4-9ef1-4d3a33a69969}}  basis.

As turns out, the phenomenon occurs always when {{formula:ac2fe90c-1bca-4f83-980a-3118c39c1b9b}}  in eq. (REF ) is symmetric with respect to the center of the operation interval. To show this it is convenient to fix the time variable {{formula:fe7cb48a-d243-429b-8b43-3d24ea181982}}  with {{formula:946942cc-9e2d-470a-8a2a-36d38cfd00fb}}  at the symmetry center; then to consider the evolution matrix {{formula:8f2f10f6-8ed1-47d8-bb50-e80a5772819a}}  generated in the expanding interval {{formula:cfaad089-d49e-4ffc-9e55-970cdce5aef1}}  (see {{cite:587d9db1c1fcda43af365367c4647fc2cf67b96c}}).

