Note that the Proposition 4 applies to the biharmonic {{formula:cac15bfc-1ca4-4d3e-b337-03bceefb0b8d}} , odd with respect to the centers of the periodicity intervals {{formula:78acccaf-22d0-4753-bf82-9157a16ce10a}} , and vanishing at the extremes {{formula:f1902453-c91d-4c92-bff1-06804a9ca6c1}}For the harmonic {{formula:65d70193-48dc-467b-903e-3743454ca354}} , the assumptions of Proposition REF  are satisfied in the periodicity intervals {{formula:a66b1dac-9f70-4c7d-8934-02c210d1ac66}}  thanks to the symmetry of {{formula:7f78e64e-300b-415e-b57f-43d793b84749}} . However, due to the existence of the constant field component, the vector potential {{formula:6bafc575-fc14-4e33-be10-d9d39485108a}} re does not vanish at the operation extremes, affecting the physical sense of (REF ) and (REF ) (c.f. the remarks in Dodonov et al. {{cite:c5c21fc6062761666ea8a239a42e7c2ce7670af6}}).. The corresponding stability borders (Fig. ) form a sequence of closed, intersecting pairs of branches surrounding the map center. The computer scanning shows that the inner pair, intersecting at 4 points, is of type {{formula:2dcc0277-4879-4016-abaa-6d745cc97702}} , {{formula:ad0a5f18-dc1c-431c-af3b-da6d3447b52b}} , hosts the evolution matrices (REF ). The next pair, with 2 intersections, is of type {{formula:e18e938c-94ec-4eac-826c-f03f7b8aaa2f}} , hosting (REF ). In general, the 1st, 3rd, and all odd pairs are of type {{formula:15f50622-7a92-4653-a5b6-6ec5217a5d03}} , whereas all even pairs are of type {{formula:a2daafdc-eb9a-4cd6-9b1a-a477bf291e72}}  (see Fig. ). On each pair, one branch (brown) offers the soft imitations of the {{formula:a7164262-b001-4e10-a056-5b3fe63db5e0}} -kicks (with the amplitudes {{formula:2e1d1acc-e1cf-4946-be20-2c21301bd5f7}} , but {{formula:13a57322-4f8c-478d-8128-9038e7992e10}} ), while the other one (green), yields the incidents of the distorted free evolution ({{formula:6baca81e-8d5f-4ce2-b30a-463672a3cbd5}} , {{formula:7cdf9257-d9be-4a65-b496-a352617ebb97}} ). Note, that the operations on the {{formula:24042a0b-8fe3-45a3-b199-f01231c0ba00}}  branches give the clean effects after the single pulse period (or its multiples), while the operations of the {{formula:f049b190-d4e9-4486-80e4-260898426110}}  branches yield the parity free effects {{formula:53bf6ee3-5810-4d2f-905c-e6b71d50ec7e}}  or {{formula:eab4cbeb-84fd-4bd3-a9b5-b219af32dedd}}  after two pulse periods (or {{formula:d073e4f8-0e86-4999-aed9-41b2a9e4ac50}} , {{formula:63fab7c9-7f2c-4139-8226-377f3fd1083f}}  after {{formula:97c16dce-78e3-4981-9d12-e8469df05325}}  repetitions). To illustrate the details, our Fig.  reports the values of the “distorted time" {{formula:85ee5096-c778-4c91-8b1f-0dbe574d5fc7}}  along the green branches and the amplitudes of the effective oscillator kicks along the first brown branch.
{{figure:a3307b10-cdfe-4ab3-857b-37062f7fcf1b}}

Our computer scanning shows that the double period operations of the type {{formula:3018c4e7-d747-4651-9e36-8c080f53b039}}  have the exactly vanishing Floquet centers, and so, produce the stable (resistant) effects, while the operations of type {{formula:8ae9891e-f671-45e9-9f9e-ca73290f4494}}  define non-vanishing fuzzy centers and can be affected by constant external forces.

As an example, we have choosen a case of the free evolution inversion for a point on the first (negative) separatrix branch of Fig. . The effects of the “retrospective operations” after 4 double periods are illustrated by the center of a wave packet recovering in the time instants {{formula:7b303465-2484-43dc-9ecb-f9586baa69ed}}  {{formula:9bdc51a7-5b58-4758-805c-2399936e4b14}}  its past shapes (shrinking instead of spreading in some number of steps); its subsequent positions showing a sequence of shifts in the direction opposite to the initial velocity (Fig. REF ).

In turn, the possibility of simulating the {{formula:cd3ee6ee-5666-44be-b544-b6c929363fc6}}  pulses of the oscillator forces by the soft biharmonic fields (brown branches of Fig. ) is no less essential: it saves the realistic sense of all operations programmed with the help of the sharp oscillator pulses {{cite:c2202c96616f95dd125659717c056987dd08d091}}, {{cite:258f0d1dce13bca853b69a92c3fad639b05f6d24}}, {{cite:44ce2b13955788eb1f887e9db328b376ad5dba0b}}.

Below we shall not comment any particular squeezed states, but rather the squeezing operator which can deform any wave packet, no matter, is it Gaussian or not, and we shall be looking for the origin of this phenomenon in the structure of the full period evolution (Floquet) operator {{formula:273e8291-562d-4239-8898-d5923501d7bc}}  and its matrix {{formula:7aabf4af-0fad-404b-9839-2bf46dc29cbc}} .

The squeezing mechanisms were carefully examined in an ample sequence of papers, the most complete ones by Dodonov et al {{cite:c5c21fc6062761666ea8a239a42e7c2ce7670af6}}, {{cite:c834dd27ae4cf77d507921d167ceb842340193e4}}, {{cite:4b1e40da600449036d7d31bf925db4e9b29009df}}, {{cite:99cbb8e8d8fa8eb87f18494a8d8a7a01a7700159}}, {{cite:8060674121c0d956a93ec2eba008cd820da83dc4}}, {{cite:c42d0bebedfa85446d35f51e5557d1e2dc3e347e}}, {{cite:ea486eccf4b3735aa751aa652cdaa5a43695e95e}}, {{cite:44ce2b13955788eb1f887e9db328b376ad5dba0b}}, {{cite:587d9db1c1fcda43af365367c4647fc2cf67b96c}}, {{cite:0a511cd4fd9da4ca7cb15c4d3eb75eaf8719744b}}, {{cite:22bc9f04380d7eb4ffeecaa257873ee3236d6118}}, {{cite:e95bd369265d564a9134d95bc54b5625f5fd55e4}}, {{cite:649519ab70d619edf46b5aca0d9b1448b3ee3f90}}, {{cite:2acb3db9abf35acc57d74236541ed7e31c78c7dc}}; so our approach cannot dissent too much, though it can contribute with some more observations:

The use of the quadratic Hamiltonians with the time dependent {{formula:b6a18601-b881-4e7d-8843-d21d791bc247}}  is justified as the first step of the known EIH method {{cite:7eccc5f63277fa108d00e2d0815ebdb4cc795dbf}} for not too ample experiment areas.

In case of periodic {{formula:b7f3732e-7eb9-4ce8-882c-957a257f6be4}}  in the cylindrical geometry the information about the squeezing can be read from the {{formula:e67b465b-2cfc-4371-a1b8-222e0a5ca769}}  oscillatory matrix {{formula:90697a19-013f-472c-a034-08c138ace689}} , even if the time dependent field pulses do not distinguish any invariant “squeezing center”. This holds also if the magnetic field vanishes at the beginning and at the end of the operation. If {{formula:b99f763e-56be-4082-b919-1e275df02fe7}}  (dark areas), then {{formula:4fbe2f9c-472a-4273-9412-bcb5bf786607}}  are real, {{formula:4f12a710-908a-4ded-ac11-319c8144567c}} , describing a squeezing on the canonical plane. For {{formula:5cdecb56-38da-4840-82bf-32c86f0b20de}}  (type +), the transformation is a squeezing sensu stricto with two positive coefficients {{formula:8073e7e6-8dce-49f6-9ade-0408b920c57f}} , but if {{formula:7f8b9ba9-0662-4cb8-a990-b0bb9f71992d}}  (type -), then {{formula:0911efc8-826e-4eed-ba00-825aa7c9fc03}}  yields the superposition of squeezing and the parity transformation (see also our recent report {{cite:587d9db1c1fcda43af365367c4647fc2cf67b96c}}). The squeezed or amplified canonical observables are defined just by the (row) eigenvectors of {{formula:8c628028-4a35-4f54-aeec-64fd17a9f49b}}  (c.f. {{cite:587d9db1c1fcda43af365367c4647fc2cf67b96c}}).

Some studies dedicate special attention to the scale squeezing or expansion in which {{formula:deacac79-2fde-461b-aae3-b10fad89e884}}  is squeezed at the cost of {{formula:7abda897-da29-4f41-b329-8933efe491e5}}  or vice versa {{cite:8060674121c0d956a93ec2eba008cd820da83dc4}}, {{cite:649519ab70d619edf46b5aca0d9b1448b3ee3f90}}. Here, the cases of {{formula:12fec048-4917-4bfd-bb2b-6b946692be47}}  or {{formula:2cc060e5-132a-4038-9467-82dfda653b82}}  are quite relevant not only on the separatrix branches, but also inside of the squeezing areas. The value {{formula:53b4b55b-7c2a-4508-9436-c5f5c4788a1c}}  in {{formula:2dd5ed05-a44d-4d44-878f-a7ee46dc2a39}}  implies the coordinate {{formula:832bd766-c2e4-4f60-a334-c5350012b2c1}}  squeezed or expanded at the cost of some other canonical observable, while {{formula:0058bd81-612e-4d15-b71c-5c96fee5ca13}} , the same for the canonical momentum. The coincidence {{formula:8ee2b490-b31f-44be-af2d-397ce634f301}} , would represent the position {{formula:f724e40f-b773-44f8-bcb4-26a0400b25f6}}  (meaning {{formula:7e0186e0-aa3f-49ea-98b6-10fb5fc8908f}}  and/or {{formula:681b5634-78e9-421d-8e61-f74ad259f659}} ) squeezed or amplified exactly at the cost of the corresponding momentum ({{formula:9f3cbaef-f6b4-47d1-ab24-d7b47f5de480}}  or {{formula:3d3f5776-b029-42b8-ae5d-3ecb1f15296e}} ). However, due to our Proposition 4, none of these cases can happen if {{formula:6600593c-5a50-4f36-9569-6664ab9da2c5}}  is symmetric around the operation center, when the desired zeroes of {{formula:dfe0c9d2-4d00-42e0-a8c3-573087122fd0}}  and/or {{formula:b151e6cd-f428-4d10-8cc0-ac71803e701a}}  are all located on the separatrix, without penetrating ever inside the dark areas. Hence, the scale transformations can be achieved only in the operation intervals with an asymmetric {{formula:909bd650-c9a9-44e2-bd7e-25410fed8163}} . In this last case the role of the `fuzzy points' again calls attention. If the Floquet center {{formula:4519d2ab-53f1-4a51-96dc-672e3b142ba4}}  of the classical/quantum trajectory vanishes, then the squeezing operations will be immune to the constant external force {{formula:02ccab8b-0a93-473f-af8b-6a92d36d7455}} . However, should {{formula:72ee70d9-5ff9-47ab-ba51-3ed3c61ed6b5}}  be fuzzy, then if the field pulses are repeated, the {{formula:e5ede603-7242-4d71-bb56-24049b4a56b0}} -trajectory, apart of the sequence of the squeezing operations, will show a drift in a direction orthogonal to F. So, would the “fuzzy center” be indeed a generalized element of noncommutative geometry applicable for variable fields?

Since the effects which we discussed are computed in the dimensionless field variables, it is not excluded that their analogues can reappear in different orders of magnitudes as {{formula:fb601640-59a7-4eea-86d6-9b6176112431}}  interiors of the oscillating cristal lattices, the nodal points of the crossed electromagnetic waves {{cite:6ce91a6a3cbd9b7cdfda86352122d365f8c9bd10}}, {{cite:258f0d1dce13bca853b69a92c3fad639b05f6d24}}, which do not involve the macroscopic traps {{cite:c85eafd5b29179ee2f679156c20116537c6e7013}}. Supposing that some analogues `in little' can exist, they might contribute to different topics recently studied.

The behavior of particle gas in {{formula:39600241-1ed6-4789-87f6-8b4cbe776897}}  in presence of the harmonic or biharmonic fields, shows a sequence of surprising reactions to the external electric force, in form of a “giant” {{cite:3d9cc20364204ba346ab113635a467697c622f05}} or vanishing, or even negative resistance {{cite:f9d06506855fe5bb50aa85c446fd63c59faf2d27}}. The effects are apparently due to the presence of material shells forming a sandwich on both sides of the motion plane; but the theoretical discussions are not yet concluded (see e.g. Zudov et al. {{cite:181f75a61b460a8724ce408794e2eb62d3afcb2b}}). One might henceforth ask, whether the surprising phenomena have not some elementary counterparts for time dependent fields? Could e.g., the behavior of the {{formula:3db7fc31-12b9-456a-9dea-a95956b2cf4d}}  evolution loops with the vanishing `fuzzy center', be interpreted as an analogue of an `anomalous resistance'? Could the distorted free evolution mean a modified effective time? Could the squeezing effects matter? Last not least, could the analogous phenomena affect the polarized vacuum, if such an entity exists {{cite:876e49a2200a09f9e9704dbc1c346dc444d1b568}}?

