The dimensionless time {{formula:9e6eb1e2-d0fd-43a1-aabe-9b71ec3e2d61}}  for a grain to reach {{formula:9b63d469-7c51-4e1f-9a54-5ede61fa01e4}}  is given by Eq. (REF ). In combination with Eq. (REF ), this gives an expression of {{formula:f67c360f-4e4d-4db3-b81a-e123b545ad3a}}  as a function of {{formula:1b572d90-e171-4b38-ac6d-5366717e063d}}  and {{formula:bfcabe81-c2d1-4b01-ab32-4040fc0fa8a5}} , whereas Eq. (REF ) gives an expression of {{formula:048771b1-9331-4bbc-83fb-0dc579bc8043}}  as a function of {{formula:d250e21c-536f-42d0-9f03-f7cc00b0c4df}} . Equating them yields a second order equation in {{formula:e2baa598-c3c8-483c-b2e9-224da83e9500}}  as a function of {{formula:cdf2145a-3c34-44d9-958d-c29d50e3a3d5}} , which can be solved to determine under which conditions a grain reaches the disc inner edge at the end of its lifetime. Using Eq. (REF ) gives the corresponding relationship between the grain size and its initial position:
{{formula:52a819cb-6fd9-489b-b392-a7a1f1be504f}}

which is plotted as a thick line in Fig. REF . It separates the {{formula:38b24c7d-91d6-42dc-9359-58a86c2e7575}}  plane into regions in which grains reach {{formula:c9f2a5e2-670f-4892-809b-8877351a9ce4}}  and leave the disc before it dissipates ({{formula:3a7b263f-0e6e-4dfa-bdcd-5d83dca1b5cb}} ) or survive in the disc throughout its lifetime ({{formula:8c589165-faeb-4721-b13d-522b4c05b088}} ). We call this curve the survival limit.

For {{formula:2bb86376-3769-44f1-8577-b1f85421cb0c}} , illustrated by the case {{formula:afaa70b4-3706-4604-ae96-a70d55325e06}} , Fig. REF  shows as expected that most of the grains are lost during the disc lifetime. However, small and large grains initially in the outer disc survive, therefore even with this profile, the disc retains a fraction of its grain population before it dissipates. Moreover, one may expect growing grains that reach {{formula:328937aa-0f17-4f1c-80ee-3740e3c68935}}  to be inevitably accreted onto the star (unless the growth process is fast enough for grains to outgrow the fast migrating sizes before they leave the disc, see {{cite:ab277264be9c8c175309b2f1bea049383b4ceaaf}}). Such discs may not form planets, but their remaining dust content may still make them observable, although they would likely be faint.

For {{formula:e97d7417-3168-4d3a-ba39-d1fd7baeee62}} , illustrated by the case {{formula:b2e37691-6c91-4adb-ab05-af0817ec32c2}} , even though all grains fall on the star in an infinite time, some of them reach the disc's inner edge before it dissipates. On the contrary, for {{formula:de62aa9c-7836-4d4c-ad4d-d1607c3c1be3}}  AU, grains of all sizes remain in the disc. This is also the case for all (sub)micron-sized grains, whatever their initial location, as well as most of the grains up to 0.1 mm. These grains likely make the disc bright and easy to observe, since they are the grains contributing most to the disc emission at IR and submm wavelengths. A large reservoir of grains is available to participate in the planet formation process, however a firm conclusion on their survival as their size evolves would require incorporating a treatment of grain growth, as discussed in Sect. .

It should be noted that the disc used in these examples represents a lower limit, as it is low mass and very extended. A more massive disc with a smaller outer radius would have a larger surface density, and the corresponding survival limit in Fig. REF  would be shifted vertically towards larger sizes and more and more grains of larger sizes would survive.
{{figure:8032c034-9394-4d6d-8adc-0bd2c57112fe}}

The left panels of Fig. REF  show the influence of the surface density and temperature profiles on the location and shape of the survival limit curve in the {{formula:4d16e34f-f65e-4b85-a848-53c1169156c7}}  plane in the Epstein regime. Increasing {{formula:4782bf32-7c63-4796-aa39-a25474b9fd24}}  from 0 to 2 shifts the curve towards smaller radii and larger grain sizes, as well as slightly tilts it clockwise. The outer disc region in which grains of all sizes survive extends inwards, as well as the surviving population of small grains as the curve's lower branch shifts upwards and becomes flatter. On the contrary, the steepening of the curve's upper branch, confining the population of surviving large solids to the disc outer regions, is less dramatic.
Increasing {{formula:9d14e697-bcab-4891-98e2-b4e69a8d8535}}  from {{formula:ae4edd63-3968-423d-9e08-dac43a4a39f7}}  to {{formula:24f1cf4b-0205-4083-ae83-e1b59a3b4a58}}  also tilts the curve clockwards, but shifts it towards larger radii and smaller grain sizes. However, its effect is more limited than that of changing {{formula:f8f0c691-a842-483d-9eae-0ca3868af10d}} .
A disc with a steeper surface density profile and a shallower temperature profile is therefore more efficient at retaining a larger quantity of small grains and up to larger sizes. Indeed, large {{formula:b9922a53-aabd-4a78-a1a2-276005e3a190}}  and small {{formula:727ef215-a2b7-48d3-8de0-1b2a28f3b61d}}  values are required to meet the {{formula:4eeeb53f-8e6f-46d8-9165-e499a0a14555}}  criterion introduced in Sect. .

An equation very similar to Eq. (REF ) can be obtained for the linear Stokes regime by replacing {{formula:68b12ddc-5ec0-4a21-a635-d0eb4148417b}}  and {{formula:d9d487fa-3b8f-4d16-91a6-06a54c2427cd}}  by {{formula:2f104c4b-35a5-4086-a99a-486d6a2bfe0c}}  and {{formula:b038c8a4-498c-4773-ac0f-70b410cd5c64}}  (since the equation of motion has the same structure for both drag regimes, see Sect. ), and using the expression of {{formula:3d71bf5b-13fe-4f31-b47a-09b774e35a70}}  for that regime, given in Table REF . Here {{formula:9d04b1de-4bce-4310-ad5e-ab3d4f4a3309}} : the weak temperature dependence results in very little change for a large range of temperatures below or above our adopted value of {{formula:22433569-0b17-46a6-ae0c-cb37a7450e7a}}  AU{{formula:17793287-6add-4d30-bdd6-eaac3e94eb2b}}  K. The right panels of Fig. REF  show the survival limit in the Stokes regime for the different values of {{formula:ee12d31b-332f-44a2-acc9-5757976c82c1}}  (note that it no longer depends on {{formula:0a977087-9971-4b64-ab42-824e0a19b466}} ). When {{formula:00fdc427-c7c4-471a-a1c0-8cf9a920fc4b}}  increases, the curve's lower branch slides towards larger radii, making the survival of particles in the Stokes regime less and less favourable.

Given the form of Eq. (REF ) and its different expressions for each drag regime, it is not possible to compute analytically the survival limit for a grain transitioning from the Epstein to the Stokes regime as it migrates inwards. However, large values of {{formula:13185777-1dc7-4d27-adaf-4a8f039c4a48}}  and {{formula:8c56a3f4-7427-41c6-a283-1eb85245af76}} , for which the Stokes region is the largest, are not observed in protoplanetary discs (see Sect. REF ), and in practical cases the Stokes regime only applies to a small area of the {{formula:cfed2847-7b04-471a-8592-06728afdb72d}}  plane. Small grains, which are detected in disc observations at IR and sub-millimetre (submm) wavelengths, are mostly subject to the Epstein drag. We therefore focus on that regime in the following.
{{figure:a8805cdd-6ef4-4b66-9cc6-70eccb501cd2}}

Equation (REF ) can give quantitative information about the outcome of the grain population. Replacing {{formula:673647af-e72c-45ac-9fe7-11ee08c8a3bd}}  by any time {{formula:2b1054d1-34a9-4819-b638-ab9bd01f6d70}}  gives the location in the {{formula:bd455ee6-3f9d-4834-b2be-d9ce5caec369}}  plane of grains reaching the disc inner edge (at {{formula:65675f1d-4c83-415e-827e-2993c57cd59a}} ) in that time {{formula:86ea84e2-1445-473b-b81b-e9181c5addfb}} , which is therefore the survival time of those grains. Its isocontours are shown in Fig. REF  for different values of {{formula:faf3f30b-dd49-44ce-b32d-047678345c7e}}  and for {{formula:e2a41e7e-b01f-450e-9616-10c4115466aa}} . Only one value of {{formula:d5c42bcb-f5cf-4370-851d-4db825381f56}}  is shown as the {{formula:e6d69896-3d66-4dc8-89fa-7bcf2b007703}}  dependence is moderate, as can be seen from the left panels of Fig. REF . The fate of particular dust grains can easily be obtained from these figures. For example, in the context of disc observations, 1 mm grains initially at 100 AU fall on the {{formula:50d6cd76-eea6-4471-99d9-79761e673d40}}  yr contour for {{formula:92048c02-0d8c-444a-908c-fdc543072c4f}} . Their survival time decreases to a few {{formula:988af64e-263f-4aa5-b215-9fb59022f315}}  yr for {{formula:dec27588-e1b7-4126-bba2-6c1bce86f0df}} , and increases again to values larger than {{formula:e87e4f8f-bb24-437b-9889-37b27c8b7e44}}  yr as {{formula:b97bb967-42b6-4e49-ad64-567224158d5c}}  increases. At an initial position of a few hundred AU, 1 mm grains survive longer than {{formula:6b5de0a2-a4ae-4dda-84c2-9f10243922a0}}  yr for any {{formula:97f87c9b-0edc-4a52-b94a-8210b59f9ff1}} , therefore long enough to contribute to the disc emission over its entire lifetime. As noted above, such grains have longer survival times for higher {{formula:d2b6e7e8-0dee-4bf7-9b38-35499ebb755b}}  and lower {{formula:7a8250fc-af22-45bf-8d1d-14837250e0b0}}  values. As another example, in the context of planetesimal formation, the survival time of a 1 m particle initially at 1 AU is {{formula:046144ed-29d6-4df1-9b7f-6a23dfabec3a}}  yr for {{formula:d297bee7-eb57-4cf9-9db1-bfee3230ca2f}} , decreases to {{formula:c22ca4f9-f594-42b7-9097-0a6411bca8b0}}  yr for {{formula:a2d3fe58-6944-44f2-a10b-a84f20a84c79}} , and increases again to {{formula:e5303f6a-fc26-45c4-b5c3-b08523840a4d}}  yr for {{formula:867f5a97-a500-44fe-b431-1b58c3deb81e}} . The ability of such particles to remain for long enough in the disc to grow to larger sizes therefore strongly depends on the surface density profile. As a general rule, the survival of pre-planetesimals in the inner disc is favoured by small values of {{formula:966934d9-612f-434b-aa75-7157b491abca}} .
{{figure:ae7295e7-f0e0-4183-a405-99968e2aaacb}}{{figure:8643681f-a398-40a5-8049-894f5539a9fc}}

Similarly, replacing now {{formula:3d14fd64-bda5-4df1-8a9a-db778ef40111}}  with any radius {{formula:9f9f6667-c274-4553-9e25-c816efc1eacc}}  in Eq. (REF ) gives the locus in the {{formula:60e61c04-2480-4b90-8cee-3065f771ed8d}}  plane of grains reaching that radius {{formula:298be6e6-2f4b-4453-9aa7-ae73bd71bdc4}}  at any time {{formula:cb231059-e6eb-4d5a-b2b6-592f00a74d09}} . Alternatively, one can plot isocontours of the initial position {{formula:30077a14-6263-42d9-8b0c-1668c07a2cb8}}  of grains in the {{formula:b7d81f1b-223f-4931-97b8-6d63bde0f5e0}}  plane at various times, thus showing the radial evolution of grains with the same initial position but different sizes. This is shown in Fig. REF  for ({{formula:562f2270-98fb-44bd-b815-f62b3769af20}} , {{formula:c81fb884-8b4b-45e3-855b-bfce2224ba33}} ) and Fig. REF  for ({{formula:751ce850-72dd-497f-ba16-22e4fa9e1389}} , {{formula:84dd1469-b4e7-442f-a25d-adc91a535fac}} ). These plots make it easy to compare the radial evolution of any particle to any physical timescale of interest in the disc. In particular, they show that the disc still contains particles at all radii at the end of its evolution ({{formula:dcb577d9-2ac5-48f0-bdce-346d0badf2d3}}  yr). No grains are found to the right of the {{formula:c3c4a4cb-f8a3-4d0b-b8b7-8486262ffb3c}}  AU contour, since this was the initial outer disc radius. In the disc with {{formula:bce987ac-33b6-4797-a5f2-5d595c35ba4f}} , no grains between {{formula:bebfaaf4-5f9e-4367-83af-d4b4b65b659c}}  and {{formula:ddc0634d-cc00-4a22-add7-c00900bdd764}}  mm remain, and grains of other sizes still present were initially in the outer disc. Given that the grains of sizes which contribute to IR and submm emission have come from a small fraction of the initial disc, this disc is likely faint. In the disc with {{formula:8a1a2a19-b2d6-42c9-a07c-20204f1d644f}} , only grains with {{formula:b874d5ea-de9a-4321-8e87-22b54236c180}}  mm are absent from the very outer regions, and the observable grains come from a larger portion of the disc, likely making the disc brighter than in the previous case.

As a conclusion, the analytic criteria derived above apply even when taking into account the finite lifetime (or inner radius) of the disc. For most CTTS discs, the dust is in the Epstein drag regime (except for some extreme values for the grains sizes and discs profiles). Therefore, the grain's radial outcome is given by the value of {{formula:e9ca8e56-f48d-4041-87c0-e512623e7ec3}} . However, the transition between discs for which the radial-drift barrier occurs or not consists more of a continuum around the value {{formula:eebff950-c63e-4753-ace6-e7879d56cf3e}}  than in the sharp transition predicted by the analytic model. Therefore, the radial motion of the grains has to be studied on a case-by-case basis for discs close to the transition {{formula:bf35fc4c-cb21-4365-8db2-b5a62a1daa60}} , using the figures shown above in this section.

We now turn to observed discs and check if they meet our Epstein and Stokes criteria to determine whether the radial-drift barrier is constraining for planet formation. To estimate the values of the {{formula:ef12e746-2279-42c4-9872-01d12f50b96b}}  and {{formula:5638f945-3486-44aa-a893-bbc4868c4de7}}  exponents for real discs, we use the results of disc modeling obtained by {{cite:5bc462406d09017a7cd7addcbf8060b7dc8463e3}}, {{cite:57e78246c573c9a213bfc408c9fde0c4b82ef4ec}} from data on 63 discs in {{formula:4404a5f2-53d5-4e0f-bc1d-0d4da81e50b7}}  Ophiuchi, Taurus and Aurigae. Using sub-millimetre fluxes measured at several wavelengths, they fit a range of disc parameters assuming a geometrically thin irradiated disc with opacities from {{cite:848792c7ede3ffee80741f0aaf0b341114ce8edc}}, a gas-to-dust ratio of 100, a disc radius of 100 AU and zero disc inclination. The temperature exponent {{formula:172bc1d9-d074-451e-84f5-cbf8e6edd8b0}}  is well constrained by the observational data set: the histogram of most probable {{formula:3b37a2cd-daff-4606-ad88-e66da848c939}}  values is shown in Fig. REF .
{{figure:f340241f-3e10-428c-8af5-4b0f20688d7e}}

However, {{formula:abc06660-29c5-406b-bbfc-3d79cd024a7e}}  is not well constrained and is usually assumed to be {{formula:31d6ac0e-cb52-4a5f-b81b-05e94f0b3805}} . Very flat profiles with {{formula:04f45e7d-8345-45e7-9122-7493ae41f8ba}}  and very steep profiles with {{formula:ba1e4e73-3579-4eae-92f8-acfc05a705da}}  seem to be excluded {{cite:63e20480acbe6cf622693c9f6ea07d0bd1d019ed}}, {{cite:44326b8798f91bb467c967f6c2bb523ec58d79ce}}, {{cite:24519a3f474c3c2c3deb5e7f8d846ec7d1880f0e}}, {{cite:d371128ae63c73829822f04aec0a25ffd133b7d2}}, {{cite:4b22892d5ef1c3303a06710f7bd3ee3a69554545}}, {{cite:57e78246c573c9a213bfc408c9fde0c4b82ef4ec}}, {{cite:e791ad9cf8d0575636f3fa13795bccbf7fc7768d}}. We represent the disc distribution modeled by Andrews & Williams from observations in the {{formula:2c08f534-14f9-494f-9b4e-ee1b563bc31d}}  diagram of Fig. REF : the histogram of Fig. REF  is represented by the gray-shaded area and spread over a range of {{formula:bc111c24-654f-490d-9724-c87e5a51804d}}  values, taking into account that extreme values of {{formula:9914b56a-e2b4-4c6c-8b7c-d7d71040f70a}}  are less probable. The dashed line ({{formula:ab3e3140-0640-4421-aef6-16621ac94b9f}} ) represents the border between migration in an infinite time and accretion onto the central star for the A-mode of migration in the Epstein regime, while the thick dotted line ({{formula:e737494e-35eb-4708-b022-afa5835bf73c}} ) represents that same border for the B-mode of migration in the Stokes regime at low Reynolds number. The two black circles indicate the discs used as examples in Sect. REF .
{{figure:921173b8-05b1-475b-b517-a5e25c050053}}

We have split the disc distribution in four regions in the {{formula:5031a8f9-af1c-40c9-8b00-34a83a588203}}  plane:

region 1: {{formula:d66012f3-2a72-47ce-8c42-cdbf8137f325}}  and {{formula:f1929708-98e7-4cd5-ba5f-a20b11d54645}} : both small and large grains experience the pile-up effect. Those discs are potentially observable and may favour planet formation.

region 2: {{formula:8de853fe-7698-456d-967f-51fe67b15dca}}  and {{formula:26ec6905-9bc9-4aa8-b880-ea409edd5296}} : only small grains experience the pile-up effect: even though such discs retain their small grains, the population of pre-planetesimals in the disc inner regions may efficiently be accreted onto the central star (at least until they reach the high-{{formula:7b69f7fc-ca1b-4201-8996-4cae389420a3}}  Stokes regime).

region 3: {{formula:52d96082-73f9-4ac2-af54-9d0e01768f8e}}  and {{formula:d0433e0f-c7de-4b27-8690-11fd34b6e07a}} : if the pre-planetesimals can form before the entire distribution of small grains has been accreted onto the central object, they will remain in the disc and may constitute planet embryos.

region 4: {{formula:920cbaba-88a9-4e85-9a36-c9531519f948}}  and {{formula:0e96d32b-fe10-4638-adfc-055b76a2a06a}} : both small and large grains are accreted onto the central star.

The Epstein criterion indicates that for {{formula:76343bfb-eb98-4cb3-bbf5-72b6d4180eeb}}  values in the range constrained by observations, discs which keep their small grain population, and are therefore likely to be bright in the IR and submm, should have {{formula:96aed7e6-1d64-4f2c-9860-201849b7460d}}  values approximately in the {{formula:9502fc59-7063-4c77-8474-45cdddaf63d7}}  range. This is indeed what is found in most disc surveys {{cite:a071a129c620db7434d2dab41d11518781d3c5db}}, {{cite:de9cdfb808b88dcac5af12820ef691e34bbbf9ec}}. On the contrary, smaller {{formula:885bb932-0811-4f0a-8e40-c1e905f3cf1b}}  values should correspond to discs which lose most of their small grains, and are therefore more difficult to detect. This is what is found by {{cite:0eaa693b8b1d33cb3c8244677c1aaf401e67ec30}}, who pushed their previous observations of the Ophiuchus star forming region {{cite:e791ad9cf8d0575636f3fa13795bccbf7fc7768d}} down to fainter discs, finding for this new sample a median {{formula:9de5dab4-63e0-44e2-bfa6-61099e26b8de}}  value of 0.9, lower than for brighter discs. The criterion we derive in this paper for small grains in the Epstein regime provides therefore the correct behaviour for explaining the range of {{formula:0da78367-74a1-4ecf-b755-a1f234028ff4}}  values of observed discs. However, this result has to be considered carefully for two reasons. Firstly, the {{formula:42d3be67-88a1-4f20-8f29-ff9eb86f9ee5}}  and the {{formula:f905b3d7-d56b-4cbe-b3dd-353ab60be381}}  exponents determined from the observations have to be considered with their respective errors. Given these uncertainties, one may not be able to distinguish between a strict negative or positive value for {{formula:ccd2b2e7-7097-49eb-9e25-1f6767705d61}} . Second, the boundary between the different zones of the {{formula:65f149ac-0455-4ba0-b1ee-edbbd63ff4a8}}  plane consists more of a continuum rather than a strict limit due to the finite lifetime/inner radii of the discs. The outcome of the grains may thus not be predicted when the value of {{formula:9d9579ef-0650-4866-bc24-eb03970a6a37}}  is close to zero.

Now turning to the Stokes criterion for large solids, Figs. REF  and REF  show that the vast majority of observed protoplanetary discs have shallow temperature profiles ({{formula:350bfb52-c758-4949-97c5-f6424600a607}} ) and are thus able to retain their population of pre-planetesimals. These discs are therefore relevant places to find evidence of planet formation, provided small grains can efficiently grow to form pre-planetesimals. For the remainder of the disc population, the outcome of pre-planetesimals will likely depend on their ability to reach the high Reynolds number Stokes regime. However, the case of a steep radial temperature profile can be encountered in at least one particular situation: circumplanetary discs which typically have temperature profiles with {{formula:d00dfdef-1e10-4a5c-814b-022b989c3115}}  {{cite:d08af49f58830e250a11649fa05c202490a60e81}}. In this environment, we predict from our Stokes criterion that planetesimals will be accreted onto the planet. The timescale of the planet formation by the core-accretion process, which usually corresponds to the time required to release the gravitational energy of the accreted bodies {{cite:2064c17ad6df7603dc48a4f20fd5826e934bcecc}}, is thus increased as the drag from the gas onto the planetesimals releases an additional thermal contribution.

In this study, we have generalised the radial grain motion studies of Weidendust1977, Nakagawa1986 and YS2002 for both the Epstein and the Stokes regimes, taking into account the effects of both the surface density and temperature profiles in the disc. As observations do not provide direct information about the three dimensional structure of discs, radial profiles of surface density and temperature are often described by power laws: {{formula:93137bb4-2e75-4510-ae3f-abe006b8ed54}}  and {{formula:104c3c13-6c19-49f1-9439-583b3d1ed1e8}} , where both {{formula:da701192-b4b1-488f-a028-4fd22f5b07b8}}  and {{formula:98199943-05e8-4c3f-a37a-bcc3a62def4d}}  take positive values. The radial dust behaviour in those discs is governed by the competition between gravity and gas drag. The final outcome of the radial motion is set by two counterbalancing effects. First, the temperature increases when the radius decreases. Consequently, the deviation from the Keplerian velocity increases, which accelerates the dust's radial inward motion. At the same time, the surface density also increases, which increases the gas drag efficiency and slows down the dust motion. The competition between these two effects fixes the ultimate mode of migration of the grain (A-mode, where the drag dominates or B-mode, where the gravity dominates) and thus the final outcome for the dust motion. In this work, we have shown that it can be represented by an analytical criterion which depends on the drag regime. For the Epstein drag regime (in which the ultimate radial motion is in the A-mode), if {{formula:45b14699-1b0b-4cf6-8516-d0272cbc63fa}} , the dust particle is accreted onto the central star in a finite time, and if {{formula:7033ac68-a2d9-477a-a6de-03a328b2192c}} , the grain pile-up results in an infinite accretion time and small dust grains remain in the disc. We have shown that, as expected, these conclusions are somewhat mitigated when taking into account the finite disc lifetime and finite disc size. However, the outcomes still remain similar: the bulk of the small grain population is lost to the star in the first case, whereas in the second case the disc keeps most of its small grains. A similar criterion is found for the Stokes regime at low Reynolds number: if {{formula:390d4a53-31d3-4245-b941-d6e5e183afc6}} , the accretion time is infinite and large pre-planetesimals remain in the disc and can constitute the primary material for planet formation. However, the Stokes radial motion differs from the Epstein regime as the ultimate radial motion occurs in the B-mode.

The observational consequence is that discs with a large population of small grains should be strong emitters in the infrared and sub-millimetre and should be easier to observe, and that those having lost most of their small dust should be fainter and harder to detect. This is indeed what is found: a large fraction of the observed discs have large {{formula:47ea411d-99e1-4ba0-9f5a-111b48c58d78}}  values whereas fainter discs tend to have lower {{formula:d428ad1b-7b8d-4124-8b40-d7e745f54d32}}  values {{cite:0eaa693b8b1d33cb3c8244677c1aaf401e67ec30}}, in agreement with this Epstein criterion. In addition, most of the observed discs have {{formula:9019398d-e5bb-4b5c-ac45-02f38b28992c}} , allowing them to retain also their large pre-planetesimals. As noted by {{cite:a071a129c620db7434d2dab41d11518781d3c5db}}, {{cite:de9cdfb808b88dcac5af12820ef691e34bbbf9ec}}, explaining the data requires a mechanism halting or slowing down the radial migration of dust grains. We show here that local pressure maxima need not be invoked, but rather that the combination of adequate surface density and temperature profiles is sufficient. The {{formula:4e2f101f-7fe4-467b-9522-7ae2a7d42a86}}  and {{formula:3195a24e-e441-4dd3-b2a4-ff03ea3f55cd}}  exponents used to reach our conclusions are of course strongly dependent on the model used to fit the data. However, even varying the fitting models, a large majority of discs still satisfy both conditions {{formula:0010493e-c410-4320-8dd2-9e02cf38e003}}  and {{formula:58ba792f-bb8f-4247-9890-7eba6921487e}} . Consequently, the radial-drift barrier (or the so-called metre-size barrier when considering an MMSN disc) does not appear to constitute a problem for planet formation for the discs that we do observe.

Our conclusions presented in this study assumed that the grain size remains constant during its motion. However, observations tell us that grains do grow {{cite:d371128ae63c73829822f04aec0a25ffd133b7d2}}, {{cite:3b4f770d53c5bd39c5332a2f32faac4fab788b61}}, {{cite:cb8e1856a02b34a55f5b28b3042c69ff9d8cda31}}, {{cite:f3b757f8ec3fc4e6ce578dc5df2c4a4fc9d9c573}}, {{cite:af78285c183b47008c5209cd072b067a9313d7b9}}. Grain growth is studied in various theoretical studies {{cite:08b2bced1814363a5f4e3361c8bcd4f826f287b5}}, {{cite:8f0daa8fb0571da15b6c2832c21f0d4611f0f55e}}, {{cite:32c22d01554467c23c5c13198355af840841719e}}, {{cite:d2ca426991e51ca758d5c069166a6db8feba2f88}}, {{cite:efb7eecf5aabfffe160c28b76ea0b7b14eec1922}}, {{cite:03df56528c5eacd438049b214e9d69b350530df4}}, {{cite:6896320110fa1c83ec218c145cddf847672fec67}}, {{cite:63517d4fa233f717ba6823e48bbe9270fac5ec5a}}, {{cite:ab277264be9c8c175309b2f1bea049383b4ceaaf}}, {{cite:4f79305fb4a3618ea129562b06d174b7a674b851}}. In a forthcoming paper, we will generalise the formalism developed here to explain the radial and vertical behaviour of growing dust grains.

