One of the most widely-known classical geometry problems is the so-called isoperimetric problem, one equivalent variation of which is:

If a figure in the plane has area {{formula:b909f23a-3148-4c7a-9522-48c70fc49340}} , what is the smallest possible value for its perimeter?

In the Euclidean plane, the optimal configuration is a circle, implying that any figure with area {{formula:79248537-0ee7-48a2-b3f9-ba889993bbad}}  has perimeter at least {{formula:2fc6cf8b-dae0-43be-a251-31e1bfd6f3c7}} , and this lower bound may be obtained if and only if the figure is a circle.

In 2011, Miller et al. {{cite:a0e223e2eae00b73aaf37cf660192a27364485f3}} extended the isoperimetric problem in a new direction, in which integer subsets took the role of geometric figures. For any integer subset {{formula:42376395-39b2-4bc9-ad21-5b723fd9bc28}} , they defined its volume as the sum over all its elements, and they defined its perimeter as the sum of all elements {{formula:0fb25d84-ed19-4d3c-aac3-37f7cd83353e}}  such that {{formula:ed935977-73ee-4da0-97f0-a2c2224442aa}} . Thus, the volume can be thought of as the sum of all the elements of {{formula:64b8a9cb-1987-49b3-9f33-63f04582e19c}} , and the perimeter can be thought of as the sum of all the elements on the “boundary" of {{formula:6afddef8-07ae-47ab-9e3f-40735cf775dc}}  (that is to say, the elements of {{formula:3936db38-caf5-452a-9413-dc68bcff1e89}}  whose successor and predecessor are not both in {{formula:9ec0a044-1878-41e7-bef3-837b3a320bad}} ).

The main focus of {{cite:a0e223e2eae00b73aaf37cf660192a27364485f3}} was to examine the relationships between a set's perimeter and its volume. More specifically, the authors wanted to answer the corresponding “isoperimetric question"They focused on this question in particular because it turns out that all of the related extremal questions are trivial.:

If a subset of {{formula:090d9feb-b784-4ec8-b8d6-740d33f00018}}  has volume {{formula:96b70e32-d8d8-4bf8-9215-68946aa4755e}} , what is the smallest possible value for its perimeter?

Adopting their notation, we will let {{formula:7f5446fc-6483-4100-8043-554a6d488752}}  denote this value through the duration of this paperThis is sequence A186053 in OEIS..

Because their work is so recent, Miller et al. are the only ones who have published on this variation of the isoperimetric problem or on the function {{formula:d913a3bf-69ee-49e7-8513-7688e5848d58}} . Their work was to provide bounds for {{formula:7aff9747-16b6-46c1-9808-5b33c0fc22db}} , by which they were able to determine its asymptotic behavior. Specifically, their main result was

