We conclude by discussing applications for computing {{formula:fcd3327f-a27d-437e-92ae-5fa63461ee79}}  and {{formula:5f1e0f3c-09b0-474b-85a9-8a8cc7e02902}}  and by listing some open questions.

In Proposition  REF, we state results that hold for all sufficiently large values of {{formula:0758d497-caef-46f2-8521-67455a2910aa}}  (in particular, for all {{formula:6ef99b8f-5dd3-4141-b9cf-d9e57f218063}} ). We then use this result to prove Theorem  REF, and we use a computer aided search to completely classify all counterexamples, which brings up a brief discussion of algorithms.

The most naïve approach to compute {{formula:d4f2714d-6f64-4f73-b730-44127a8bb9d1}}  would be simply to list all sets of volume {{formula:767e9963-18b3-472f-bc0b-b24a95e59323}}  and find which has the smallest perimeter. This would require roughly {{formula:739c46a7-39a3-45f4-9746-6c7ee2653472}}  time and {{formula:f8149290-f06f-4969-ac1e-051f83ff08cc}}  memory, which is much too slow for large {{formula:f11a0b21-b112-4cbb-ba46-47bbdbf188e4}} , and a different approach is needed.

Using the recurrence relations in Section , dynamic programming enables us to design algorithms for computing {{formula:68feb2c2-dd8d-41ff-acce-0ffa327bd18e}}  and {{formula:b216bfcf-1149-4973-8724-f0a154ce6018}}  taking {{formula:6537e1b4-7509-4e09-9e41-f7c024458ca3}}  time and using {{formula:edcee0da-f67e-445d-a45e-ec52e127bf75}}  memory. We can reduce this memory requirement to roughly {{formula:245d893d-3775-4d04-9585-33657e197982}}  by employing a custom data structure, which benefits from the fact that for fixed {{formula:f3108890-1d0c-4be8-baf9-3f43b20ae69a}} , functions such as {{formula:738aeaa4-e412-40f2-9be5-cc2e791fc817}}  seem to take very few distinct values. Using these algorithms, the author was able to check all values of {{formula:8e464806-d7f5-4f2e-a25d-c3ac727f869e}}  and {{formula:06b2da2f-c4ce-4e86-9eb9-3e4d5e25a002}}  for {{formula:fbfcc6e0-1a52-4ca1-bb2b-618076baddac}} , which is more than enough to obtain the results of Theorem  REF.

Now that we have proven the recurrences in Theorem  REF and Proposition  REF, we may use these to compute {{formula:9ea2d542-164c-444d-b5f0-767560a37327}}  or {{formula:ab77c79c-72d9-4028-9ac4-4ae6bc34fea1}}  in {{formula:7ab042b7-e370-4f71-bd3b-533efa6eb2e4}}  time using no additional memory. Moreover, we can compute a list of {{formula:2feaed2d-9526-4e01-8ace-38de8ee9b424}}  [or {{formula:ceaee20d-a680-4430-9c18-0287285c43c4}} ] in {{formula:9c9c7dbe-7e1f-4e1c-ba0d-147efe515384}}  time using the required {{formula:caed3ba0-b442-41d5-8e7a-7b60136d5c31}}  memory.

Thus, one can now simply use Theorem  REF and the 177 values in Table  REF to compute {{formula:c42c7e71-e091-49c2-a54a-b9f4c939ab93}}  and {{formula:6dc68f44-6199-46d7-bc0a-c2edb1cd3b06}}  extremely quickly, and {{formula:f55e8c61-8065-4383-9612-9cbe78f607a2}}  and {{formula:3d8b58e6-e640-4a0c-b1f6-abc8ba1ed9b7}}  can be tabulated essentially as far out as desired. The author is more than willing to provide anyone interested with code and calculated results.

There are several possible areas of future research. Because the function {{formula:1b97633a-245c-4770-84dd-422e5ff0ace0}}  was first introduced so recently, this paper serves as a comprehensive overview of all that is known.

–
Little is known about the behavior of the functions {{formula:bd7d0363-3804-4d2c-9e67-784130a2bbfb}} , {{formula:88af0c45-71e7-4a00-972d-cbf35517e768}} , and {{formula:5e2f5929-297c-4f0f-91a1-7b030eb73b2c}} .
–
It appears that for any fixed {{formula:396e5c18-d084-4f9e-bb2b-a23061dfd3b4}}  the function {{formula:e360e16b-2138-4b01-9341-8a9c5baaf909}}  takes at most two finite values as {{formula:dc0d7f2c-e7d1-47f8-9f28-34f8fe1b5213}}  varies. This may be interesting and might be proveable by focusing on Proposition  REF.
–
Very little or nothing whatsoever is known about {{formula:56ff2193-a7a7-4e1e-b5a1-49a3428b2347}}  from Proposition  REF.
–
Characterizing sets for which {{formula:c2eb227a-a262-4cd4-a6b8-1d7bc6f7c0e9}}  is obtained may be interesting. It seems likely that the partitions used and the code developed in this paper would help with that. Moreover, the result of Theorem  REF seems likely to help with this.
–
Providing more direct (i.e., less analytic) proofs for these results would likely be quite enlightening.
–
There seems to be no pattern or unifying properties for the 177 counterexamples tabulated in Table  REF. Alternate proofs of the main results may shed light on these seemingly sporadic values.

